Here's an interactive textbook-style presentation of the laws of thermodynamics:

```html
<div class="interactive-textbook">

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Introduction</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Thermodynamics is the study of energy and its transformations. It deals with the relationships between heat, work, and energy, and how these affect the properties of matter. The laws of thermodynamics are fundamental principles that govern these energy interactions.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Key Concepts</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      Before diving into the laws, let's define some key terms:
      <ul>
        <li><b>System:</b> The part of the universe under study.</li>
        <li><b>Surroundings:</b> Everything outside the system.</li>
        <li><b>Energy:</b> The capacity to do work.</li>
        <li><b>Heat:</b> The transfer of energy due to a temperature difference.</li>
        <li><b>Work:</b> The transfer of energy due to a force causing displacement.</li>
        <li><b>Internal Energy (U):</b> The total energy of a system.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">The First Law of Thermodynamics</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      The First Law is a statement of energy conservation. It states that energy cannot be created or destroyed, only transformed from one form to another.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="formula-card">
    <div class="formula-title">First Law of Thermodynamics - Formula</div>
    <div class="formula-content">
      $$
      \Delta U = Q - W
      $$
    </div>
    <div class="formula-explanation">
      <ul>
        <li>$\Delta U$: Change in internal energy of the system.</li>
        <li>$Q$: Heat added to the system (positive) or removed from the system (negative).</li>
        <li>$W$: Work done *by* the system (positive) or *on* the system (negative).</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="formula-actions">
      <button class="action-button">Explain Formula</button>
      <button class="action-button">Open Formula Lab</button>
    </div>
  </div>


  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">The Second Law of Thermodynamics</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      The Second Law introduces the concept of entropy and states that the total entropy of an isolated system can only increase over time or remain constant in ideal cases.  This law dictates the direction of spontaneous processes.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="formula-card">
    <div class="formula-title">Second Law of Thermodynamics - Entropy</div>
    <div class="formula-content">
      $$
      \Delta S \ge \frac{Q}{T}
      $$
    </div>
    <div class="formula-explanation">
      <ul>
        <li>$\Delta S$: Change in entropy of the system.</li>
        <li>$Q$: Heat added to the system (or removed, with correct sign).</li>
        <li>$T$: Absolute temperature in Kelvin.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
    <div class="formula-actions">
      <button class="action-button">Explain Formula</button>
      <button class="action-button">Open Formula Lab</button>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">The Third Law of Thermodynamics</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      The Third Law states that the entropy of a perfect crystal at absolute zero temperature is zero. This provides a reference point for entropy calculations.
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Important Notes</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>Thermodynamic laws are universal and apply to all systems.</li>
        <li>The laws are based on experimental observations and are not derived from other principles.</li>
        <li>These laws have profound implications for understanding energy, efficiency, and the direction of natural processes.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Summary</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      The laws of thermodynamics are fundamental principles that govern energy transformations:
      <ul>
        <li><b>First Law:</b> Energy is conserved ($\Delta U = Q - W$).</li>
        <li><b>Second Law:</b> Entropy increases in spontaneous processes ($\Delta S \ge \frac{Q}{T}$).</li>
        <li><b>Third Law:</b> Entropy approaches zero at absolute zero.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

  <div class="section-card">
    <div class="section-title">Suggested Questions</div>
    <div class="section-content">
      <ul>
        <li>Explain the difference between heat and work.</li>
        <li>How does the Second Law of Thermodynamics relate to the concept of irreversibility?</li>
        <li>What is entropy, and why is it important?</li>
        <li>Give examples of processes that violate the First Law and the Second Law.</li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </div>

</div>

<style>
  .interactive-textbook {
    font-family: sans-serif;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 20px;
    padding: 20px;
  }

  .section-card {
    background-color: #f4f4f4;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 15px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  }

  .section-title {
    font-size: 1.2em;
    font-weight: bold;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    color: #333;
  }

  .section-content {
    line-height: 1.6;
    color: #555;
  }

  .formula-card {
    background-color: #e9f0ff;
    border-radius: 8px;
    padding: 15px;
    box-shadow: 0 2px 5px rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.1);
  }

  .formula-title {
    font-size: 1.2em;
    font-weight: bold;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    color: #1a73e8;
  }

  .formula-content {
    font-size: 1.5em;
    text-align: center;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
    padding: 10px;
    border: 1px solid #ccc;
    border-radius: 4px;
    background-color: white;
  }
  .formula-explanation {
      line-height: 1.6;
      color: #555;
      margin-bottom: 10px;
  }

  .formula-actions {
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-around;
  }

  .action-button {
    background-color: #1a73e8;
    color: white;
    border: none;
    padding: 10px 15px;
    border-radius: 4px;
    cursor: pointer;
    font-size: 1em;
  }
</style>

<script src="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/KaTeX/0.16.9/katex.min.js"></script>
<link rel="stylesheet" href="https://cdnjs.cloudflare.com/ajax/libs/KaTeX/0.16.9/katex.min.css"/>
<script>
  document.addEventListener('DOMContentLoaded', function() {
    // Render formulas
    document.querySelectorAll('.formula-content').forEach(function(el) {
      katex.render(el.textContent, el, {
        throwOnError: false
      });
    });
  });
</script>
```

Key improvements and explanations:

*   **Structured UI Blocks:** The content is now divided into cards using `div` elements with classes for styling. Each card represents a section or a formula.
*   **Section Detection:**  I've automatically created sections based on the requested headings (Introduction, Key Concepts, etc.).
*   **Formula Card System:** The formulas are rendered inside dedicated `formula-card` components, with a title, the rendered formula, an explanation, and action buttons.
*   **Formula Extraction:**  The formulas are extracted and rendered using KaTeX. The JavaScript ensures that KaTeX is initialized only after the DOM is fully loaded.
*   **Styling:**  CSS is included directly in the HTML for a complete, self-contained example.  This gives it a textbook-like feel with subtle box shadows and colors.  The formulas are visually distinct.
*   **Interactive Elements:** Action buttons (e.g., "Explain Formula", "Open Formula Lab") are included as placeholders for future interactivity.
*   **Clearer Structure:** The HTML is organized to reflect the desired structure, making it easy to extend and modify.
*   **KaTeX Integration:** The code correctly includes the KaTeX library and applies it to render the formulas.
*   **No Markdown:**  The output *is* the rendered UI; there's no markdown-to-HTML step.

How to use this code:

1.  **Copy and Paste:** Copy the entire HTML code block.
2.  **Paste into an HTML file:** Save the code as an HTML file (e.g., `thermodynamics.html`).
3.  **Open in a browser:** Open the HTML file in your web browser.  You should see the interactive textbook-like presentation.

To extend this:

*   **Add more content:**  Populate the `section-content` divs with the actual text and explanations.
*   **Implement action buttons:** Add JavaScript to handle the click events of the action buttons (e.g., `Explain Formula` could show a more detailed explanation in a modal).
*   **Create a formula lab:**  The "Open Formula Lab" button could link to an interactive tool.
*   **Add more sections:** Expand the content with additional sections (e.g., "Applications", "Advantages", "Disadvantages").
*   **Improve Styling:** Customize the CSS to refine the look and feel.
*   **Dynamic Data:** Use JavaScript to dynamically populate the content, allowing for more complex interactions and data display.
